   "text": {
    "L1": "Water can be a solid, a liquid, or a gas. These are its three states.",
    "L2": "Ice is solid and keeps a fixed shape. Liquid water takes the shape of its container but keeps its amount.",
    "L3": "Water vapour is a gas. It is invisible and spreads to fill the space around it.",
    "L4": "When ice is warmed, it melts at 0 °C and becomes water. Water freezes into ice at 0 °C.",
    "L5": "When water boils at about 100 °C, it becomes vapour. When vapour cools, it condenses back into water.",
    "L6": "Here is a common mistake: the white cloud above boiling water is called vapour.",
    "L7": "That is not true. Vapour is invisible; the white cloud is made of tiny droplets formed when vapour cools.",
    "L8": "So water changes state, but it remains water. Can you identify the invisible gas?"
   },
